	"notes": "The Guardian's homepage stacks three bars at the very top — a subscribe banner, a strip of story teasers, and the paper's own masthead with its section links — before the news list even begins. That teaser strip is a genuine carousel: on a laptop only two stories fit before a round arrow button appears so you can see more, and on a bigger screen a third story fits and the arrow disappears entirely. Below that, the news content behaves like several other big sites captured tonight — it stretches to fill a laptop screen, but from a large desktop monitor upward it stops growing and holds at exactly 1300 pixels wide, turning the leftover room into quiet margin on both sides. One honest limitation of these particular pictures: a large 'Your privacy' consent box covers most of the actual news list in all four screenshots, so what they mostly show is the masthead and the teaser strip rather than the full front page.",
